 The chat composer: the prompt language, the editor that renders it, and
 everything between typing and sending.
+
+On desktop and web, `/btw` opens an independent native side chat.
+The empty command forks the server's latest history without sending a prompt.
+Running turns and their existing progress remain available in the snapshot.
+The first turn can still be running, and the local message cache can be empty.
+A command with a question keeps the normal preparation and send pipeline.
+Each command creates a separate session and native tab.
+The parent keeps its selected session and composer.
+The native side chat uses the same transcript and composer as the main chat.
+Every send in an active BTW fork includes the existing boundary instruction.
+Queued follow-ups capture the same instruction before delivery.
+The composer moves its pending draft before it reveals the new tab.
+The tab uses the main panel's directory even when its conversation belongs to another project.
+Native side sessions carry `btwPresentation: 'side'` so links can reopen their own tabs.
+Mobile and VS Code keep the floating dock.
+
+New BTW sessions inherit the source composer's agent, provider, model and reasoning effort.
+Fresh native drafts ignore saved selections from an older floating dock.
+The pending composer captures those source choices before the first question is sent.
+Existing side chats retain their own saved choices.
+
+The inherited agent and tool definitions stay unchanged.
+A fixed read-only policy follows the history boundary and accompanies later side-chat sends.
+It forbids file changes, Git changes, service control, background work, deployment and external writes.
+Requests for such changes produce instructions for the main chat.
+This is a prompt policy, and existing permission enforcement continues to apply.
+
+The selection menu opens a new native side-chat draft with the selected text.
+The header menu, command palette, and `open_side_chat` shortcut open an empty draft.
+The default shortcut is `mod+alt+s`.
+These entries preserve the source draft and attachments.
+A bare `/btw` transfers its attachments through the existing draft handoff.
+Opening a draft sends no model request.
+The destination composer receives focus after it mounts.
+The same destination restore focuses a side chat created with `/btw question`.
+It preserves follow-up text typed while that fork was being created.
+Pending text waits until the context zone becomes interactive.
+The source composer does not cache the destination draft.
+
+Each new fork records its creation time and its last cloned message ID in session metadata.
+The context banner shows the snapshot time and opens the parent chat.
+Forked message IDs differ from parent message IDs, so new snapshots do not invent a parent message link.
+Older forks retain their completed-answer time and source link.
+The inherited history remains reference context.
+Later messages in the parent do not automatically enter the side chat.
+
+### Why BTW forks the server head during a running turn
+
+The user can ask about the task's current progress without waiting for its turn to end.
+OpenCode copies the current history into an independent session while the parent continues running.
+The trailing boundary makes inherited work reference context for the side question.
+
+Each fork receives the public Codex history-boundary prompt once, after its inherited history.
+The read-only policy follows that public prompt and overrides its conditional permission for edits.
+The adapter appends this note with `resume: false`, including for an empty side chat.
+The first user prompt follows that note.
+Later sends append the position-independent reminder already used by existing side chats.
+The boundary does not prepend instructions or change inherited messages.
+Source IDs, timestamps, and titles stay in session metadata and the banner.
+They do not enter the boundary text.
+
+### Why selection requests create an empty fork before inserting the quote
+
+The user can add a question before sending the quote.
+The draft belongs to the new side chat, so the source keeps its text and attachments.
 
